      ],
      "text": "Làm đềtrực tuyến tại: https://ielts-fighter.com/online-test.html\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        59.94099426269531,
        302.55078125,
        76.59700012207031
      ],
      "text": "Website tựhọc IELTS: https://ielts-fighter.com\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        84.99369812011719,
        526.856689453125,
        163.43360900878906
      ],
      "text": "Strong winds from the sea make it difficult for the determined elm bark beetle\nto attack this coastal city's elm population. However, the situation is precarious.\n'The beetles can just march in if we're not careful, as the threat is right on our\ndoorstep,' says Bourne.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        176.25205993652344,
        522.9322509765625,
        354.2336120605469
      ],
      "text": "F. Any prospect of the elm returning relies heavily on trees being either\nresistant to, or tolerant of, the disease. This means a widespread\nreintroduction would involve existing or new hybrid strains derived from\nresistant, generally non-native elm species. A new generation of seedlings have\nbeen bred and tested to see if they can withstand the fungus by cutting a small\nslit on the bark and injecting a tiny amount of the pathogen. 'The effects are\nvery quick,' says Russell. 'You return in four to six weeks and trees that are\nresistant show no symptoms, whereas those that are susceptible show leaf loss\nand may even have died completely.'\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        367.0520324707031,
        513.418212890625,
        584.6336059570312
      ],
      "text": "G. All of this raises questions of social acceptance, acknowledges Russell. 'If\nwe're putting elm back into the landscape, a small element of it is not native\nare we bothered about that?' For her, the environmental case for\nreintroducing elm is strong. 'They will host wildlife, which is a good thing.'\nOthers are more wary. 'On the face of it, it seems like a good idea,' says Elliot.\nThe problem, he suggests, is that, 'You're replacing a native species with a\nhorticultural analogue*. You're effectively cloning.' There's also the risk of\nintroducing new diseases. Rather than plant new elms, the Woodland Trust\nemphasises providing space to those elms that have survived independently.\n'Sometimes the best thing you can do is just give nature time to recover over\ntime, you might get resistance,' says Elliot.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        597.7537231445312,
        514.5240478515625,
        636.4736328125
      ],
      "text": "*horticultural analogue: a cultivated plant species that is genetically similar to\nan existing species\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        684.092041015625,
        169.10899353027344,
        703.5934448242188
      ],
      "text": "Questions 14–18\n"
    },
